# Super Dodge Ball (USA) - a recorded playthrough, 16:9 widescreen session.
#
# Written by `nes_game --record-input` from a windowed session. One
# "<frame> <BUTTONS>" line per state change, absolute state, "-" is
# release -- the same schedule format `--input` replays, where frame 0
# is the first post-power frame. It records exactly the bytes the guest
# read each frame, so the replay takes the same path.
#
# REPLAY IT (the last line of this file has the frame count for
# --frames; --ram-init zeros is REQUIRED, not a default):
#
#     ./build-cycle/nes_game "roms/Super Dodge Ball (USA).nes" \
#         --frames 1459 --input tas/vectors/sdb_16x9.input --ram-init zeros
#
# The game is random, so a replay without --ram-init zeros diverges and
# this vector will not reproduce.
#
# MEASURED 2026-10-13: a headless replay of such a vector is NOT faithful
# to the windowed recording it came from. Two windowed runs of one
# schedule are byte-identical to each other, but a headless replay of the
# recorded vector diverges from both at frame 4 and ends up on different
# game state. It is not the input -- the recorded frames are the frames
# the schedule fired on, and the divergence starts four frames before the
# first press -- so the window and the headless host are simply not running
# the same machine yet. That is an open engine question (research 33).
#
# Until it is settled, analyse the recording where it was made: its SDB_TRACE
# log is the ground truth for what was on screen. A replay is fine for a
# BEFORE/AFTER comparison of two builds on the same machine, which is what
# research 35 used it for.
#
# This vector is the one the margin's split-character artefact was found on
# (research 35). The session was played at 16:9 with all three mods on.
